{"metadata":{"exam_title":"UPGDC Assistant Professor — Sociology: Contemporary Power and Surveillance, Globalization and Macro-Sociology, Deviance and Social Control, The Sociology of Religion, Social Mobility and Structural Barriers","total_duration_minutes":100,"version":"1.0","allow_backtrack":true},"sections":[{"section_id":"contemporary_power_surveillance_hard","section_name":"Contemporary Power and Surveillance","section_type":"FREE_FLOAT","questions":[{"id":1,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Consider the following statements regarding Michel Foucault's conception of power:\n1. Power is productive as well as repressive.\n2. Power is possessed permanently by a ruling class.\n3. Discourses are central mechanisms through which power operates.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?"},"options":{"A":"1 and 3 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 2 only","D":"1, 2 and 3"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Statement 1 is correct because Foucault argued that power produces knowledge, norms, and subjectivities rather than functioning only as repression. Statement 2 is incorrect because Foucault rejected the idea that power is simply possessed permanently by one class; instead, power circulates through social relations. Statement 3 is correct because discourse structures what can be said, known, and practiced. Option B is incorrect because statement 2 is false. Option C is incorrect because statement 2 remains incorrect. Option D is incorrect because all statements are not correct."},{"id":2,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Assertion (A): Foucault viewed the prison as a model institution of modern disciplinary society.\nReason (R): The prison exemplifies surveillance, normalization, and examination as techniques of control."},"options":{"A":"Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Assertion is true because Foucault considered prisons symbolic of wider disciplinary mechanisms in modern society. Reason is also true because prisons rely on surveillance, normalization, classification, and examination. These techniques explain why prisons represent disciplinary power. Option B is incorrect because the reason directly explains the assertion. Option C is incorrect because the reason is true. Option D is incorrect because the assertion is also true."},{"id":3,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Which of the following statements about biopower is NOT correct?"},"options":{"A":"It concerns the regulation of populations","B":"It emerged alongside modern states and statistical governance","C":"It focuses exclusively on punishing criminal acts","D":"It includes management of health, fertility, and mortality"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Biopower does not focus exclusively on punishment. It concerns management of populations through health policies, demography, medicine, and regulation of life processes. Option A is correct because biopower regulates populations. Option B is correct because modern administrative states expanded statistical and bureaucratic governance. Option D is correct because fertility, mortality, and public health are central concerns of biopower. Therefore option C is incorrect."},{"id":4,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Match List I with List II:\nList I\n1. Synopticon\n2. Panopticon\n3. Society of control\n4. Risk surveillance\n\nList II\na. Gilles Deleuze\nb. Thomas Mathiesen\nc. Michel Foucault\nd. Ulrich Beck"},"options":{"A":"1-b, 2-c, 3-a, 4-d","B":"1-c, 2-a, 3-d, 4-b","C":"1-d, 2-b, 3-c, 4-a","D":"1-a, 2-d, 3-b, 4-c"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Synopticon was proposed by Thomas Mathiesen to describe the many watching the few through media. Panopticon is associated with Foucault's interpretation of Bentham. Society of control is associated with Gilles Deleuze. Risk surveillance is linked with Ulrich Beck's risk society thesis. Hence option A is correct."},{"id":5,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Arrange the following developments in chronological order:\n1. Publication of 'Discipline and Punish'\n2. Jeremy Bentham's Panopticon writings\n3. Gilles Deleuze's essay on societies of control"},"options":{"A":"2-1-3","B":"1-2-3","C":"2-3-1","D":"3-1-2"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Bentham's Panopticon writings emerged in the eighteenth century. Foucault's 'Discipline and Punish' was published in 1975. Deleuze's essay on societies of control appeared later in the 1990s. Therefore the correct order is 2-1-3."},{"id":6,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Consider the following statements regarding surveillance capitalism:\n1. Personal data are treated as economic resources.\n2. Surveillance capitalism depends entirely on state ownership of digital platforms.\n3. Predictive algorithms are used to shape consumer behaviour.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?"},"options":{"A":"1 and 3 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 2 only","D":"1, 2 and 3"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Statement 1 is correct because surveillance capitalism commodifies personal data. Statement 2 is incorrect because private corporations are central actors in surveillance capitalism. Statement 3 is correct because predictive analytics are used to influence behaviour and consumption patterns. Hence only statements 1 and 3 are correct."},{"id":7,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Assertion (A): Contemporary surveillance increasingly operates through decentralized digital networks.\nReason (R): Surveillance today depends only on direct face-to-face observation."},"options":{"A":"Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Assertion is true because digital surveillance relies on distributed data systems, online platforms, and algorithmic monitoring. Reason is false because contemporary surveillance extends far beyond direct observation into automated and remote forms of monitoring. Therefore option C is correct."},{"id":8,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Which of the following best reflects Gramsci's concept of hegemony?"},"options":{"A":"Political domination achieved solely through military force","B":"Economic exploitation without ideological influence","C":"Cultural leadership through consent and ideological influence","D":"Complete elimination of dissent in society"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Gramsci argued that ruling groups maintain dominance by securing consent through culture, education, religion, and ideology. Option A overemphasizes coercion. Option B ignores ideological dimensions. Option D is unrealistic because hegemony does not require elimination of all dissent. Hence option C is correct."},{"id":9,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Consider the following statements about algorithmic governance:\n1. Algorithms can classify populations according to risk categories.\n2. Algorithmic systems are always completely objective and unbiased.\n3. Predictive policing is an example of algorithmic governance.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?"},"options":{"A":"1 only","B":"1 and 3 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Statement 1 is correct because algorithmic systems categorize individuals based on behavioural or demographic data. Statement 2 is incorrect because algorithms can reproduce social biases embedded in data and institutions. Statement 3 is correct because predictive policing uses algorithms to estimate crime risks. Therefore statements 1 and 3 are correct."},{"id":10,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Which thinker argued that contemporary societies are shifting from disciplinary institutions toward flexible 'societies of control'?"},"options":{"A":"Anthony Giddens","B":"Pierre Bourdieu","C":"Gilles Deleuze","D":"Jurgen Habermas"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Gilles Deleuze argued that contemporary capitalism increasingly operates through fluid and continuous forms of control rather than enclosed disciplinary institutions. Giddens focused on modernity and structuration. Bourdieu emphasized habitus and capital. Habermas focused on communicative action. Hence option C is correct."}]},{"section_id":"globalization_macro_sociology_hard","section_name":"Globalization and Macro-Sociology","section_type":"FREE_FLOAT","questions":[{"id":11,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Consider the following statements regarding world-systems theory:\n1. Core countries dominate peripheral countries economically.\n2. Semi-peripheral countries share characteristics of both core and periphery.\n3. The theory assumes that all nations develop through identical stages.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?"},"options":{"A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 only","D":"1, 2 and 3"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Statement 1 is correct because core countries dominate global production and trade. Statement 2 is correct because semi-peripheral regions occupy intermediate positions. Statement 3 is incorrect because identical developmental stages are associated with modernization theory, not world-systems theory. Hence statements 1 and 2 are correct."},{"id":12,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Assertion (A): Ulrich Beck argued that modern societies increasingly confront manufactured risks.\nReason (R): Technological and industrial development can generate hazards that transcend national boundaries."},"options":{"A":"Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Beck argued that advanced modernity produces global risks such as nuclear disasters and climate change. The reason is true because industrial and technological development creates transnational hazards. The reason directly explains the assertion. Therefore option A is correct."},{"id":13,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Which of the following statements about globalization is NOT correct?"},"options":{"A":"Globalization may weaken national economic autonomy","B":"Globalization necessarily produces cultural uniformity everywhere","C":"Global financial flows can affect domestic economies rapidly","D":"Migration and media networks contribute to globalization"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Globalization does not necessarily create total cultural uniformity; hybridization and localization also occur. Option A is correct because global markets constrain national policies. Option C is correct because financial integration creates rapid economic interdependence. Option D is correct because migration and media accelerate global connections. Hence option B is incorrect."},{"id":14,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Match List I with List II:\nList I\n1. Network society\n2. Time-space compression\n3. McDonaldization\n4. Risk society\n\nList II\na. George Ritzer\nb. David Harvey\nc. Manuel Castells\nd. Ulrich Beck"},"options":{"A":"1-c, 2-b, 3-a, 4-d","B":"1-b, 2-a, 3-d, 4-c","C":"1-d, 2-c, 3-b, 4-a","D":"1-a, 2-d, 3-c, 4-b"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Network society is associated with Manuel Castells. Time-space compression was developed by David Harvey. McDonaldization was proposed by George Ritzer. Risk society is associated with Ulrich Beck. Therefore option A is correct."},{"id":15,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Arrange the following theories in chronological order:\n1. Modernization theory\n2. Dependency theory\n3. World-systems theory"},"options":{"A":"1-2-3","B":"2-1-3","C":"3-2-1","D":"1-3-2"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Modernization theory gained prominence in the 1950s. Dependency theory emerged during the 1960s as a critique of modernization. World-systems theory developed later in the 1970s. Hence the correct sequence is 1-2-3."},{"id":16,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Consider the following statements regarding transnational migration:\n1. Diasporic communities can maintain ties with multiple societies simultaneously.\n2. Global migration flows are influenced only by economic factors.\n3. Remittance economies may reshape local social relations.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?"},"options":{"A":"1 only","B":"1 and 3 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Statement 1 is correct because diasporic groups often maintain social, cultural, and economic ties across borders. Statement 2 is incorrect because migration is also influenced by war, politics, environment, and family networks. Statement 3 is correct because remittances can alter status relations and consumption patterns. Hence statements 1 and 3 are correct."},{"id":17,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Assertion (A): Globalization has intensified the mobility of capital more rapidly than the mobility of labour.\nReason (R): States generally impose fewer restrictions on financial transactions than on migration."},"options":{"A":"Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Globalization has enabled rapid movement of capital across borders while labour mobility remains relatively restricted. The reason correctly explains this imbalance because states often liberalize financial markets more than migration policies. Therefore option A is correct."},{"id":18,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Which concept refers to the adaptation of global products and ideas to local cultural contexts?"},"options":{"A":"Fordism","B":"Glocalization","C":"Secularization","D":"Anomie"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Glocalization refers to the blending of global and local processes where global products are adapted to local contexts. Fordism concerns mass production. Secularization relates to declining religious authority. Anomie refers to normlessness. Hence option B is correct."},{"id":19,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Consider the following statements about neoliberal globalization:\n1. It emphasizes market deregulation and privatization.\n2. It supports extensive state control over all industries.\n3. Critics argue that it may widen social inequality.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?"},"options":{"A":"1 and 3 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 2 only","D":"1, 2 and 3"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Statement 1 is correct because neoliberalism promotes deregulation and privatization. Statement 2 is incorrect because neoliberalism favors reducing direct state economic control. Statement 3 is correct because critics argue that neoliberal reforms can increase inequality. Therefore statements 1 and 3 are correct."},{"id":20,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Which sociologist argued that globalization should be understood through the disjunctive flows of ethnoscapes, mediascapes, technoscapes, financescapes, and ideoscapes?"},"options":{"A":"Anthony Giddens","B":"Arjun Appadurai","C":"Zygmunt Bauman","D":"Immanuel Wallerstein"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Arjun Appadurai proposed the framework of multiple global 'scapes' to explain complex global cultural flows. Giddens emphasized disembedding and reflexive modernity. Bauman discussed liquid modernity. Wallerstein focused on capitalist world-systems. Hence option B is correct."}]},{"section_id":"deviance_social_control_hard","section_name":"Deviance and Social Control","section_type":"FREE_FLOAT","questions":[{"id":21,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Consider the following statements regarding labeling theory:\n1. Deviance is partly created through social reactions.\n2. Primary deviance may lead to secondary deviance.\n3. The theory places major emphasis on biological determinism.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?"},"options":{"A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 only","D":"1, 2 and 3"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Statement 1 is correct because labeling theory emphasizes how social definitions create deviance. Statement 2 is correct because repeated labeling may push individuals toward secondary deviance. Statement 3 is incorrect because labeling theory rejects biological determinism. Therefore statements 1 and 2 are correct."},{"id":22,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Assertion (A): Durkheim argued that crime can have positive social functions.\nReason (R): Reactions to crime may reinforce collective norms and solidarity."},"options":{"A":"Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Durkheim believed that crime helps clarify moral boundaries and strengthen collective conscience. The reason correctly explains how responses to crime reinforce social solidarity. Therefore option A is correct."},{"id":23,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Which of the following statements about differential association theory is NOT correct?"},"options":{"A":"Criminal behaviour is learned through interaction","B":"Association with deviant groups can influence behaviour","C":"The theory explains crime mainly through inherited instincts","D":"Definitions favourable to law violation may encourage deviance"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Differential association theory does not explain crime through inherited instincts; it emphasizes social learning. Option A is correct because criminal behaviour is learned. Option B is correct because peer groups influence deviance. Option D is correct because exposure to favourable definitions of law violation promotes deviance. Hence option C is incorrect."},{"id":24,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Match List I with List II:\nList I\n1. Anomie\n2. Differential association\n3. Techniques of neutralization\n4. Broken windows theory\n\nList II\na. Wilson and Kelling\nb. Durkheim\nc. Sykes and Matza\nd. Edwin Sutherland"},"options":{"A":"1-b, 2-d, 3-c, 4-a","B":"1-d, 2-c, 3-a, 4-b","C":"1-c, 2-a, 3-b, 4-d","D":"1-a, 2-b, 3-d, 4-c"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Anomie is associated with Durkheim. Differential association theory was proposed by Edwin Sutherland. Techniques of neutralization were developed by Sykes and Matza. Broken windows theory was proposed by Wilson and Kelling. Therefore option A is correct."},{"id":25,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Arrange the following theories chronologically according to their development:\n1. Strain theory\n2. Labeling theory\n3. Differential association theory"},"options":{"A":"1-3-2","B":"3-1-2","C":"2-1-3","D":"1-2-3"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Merton's strain theory developed in the 1930s. Differential association theory became influential afterward. Labeling theory emerged prominently in the 1960s. Hence the correct sequence is 1-3-2."},{"id":26,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Consider the following statements regarding social control:\n1. Formal social control relies on codified rules.\n2. Informal social control can operate through shame and approval.\n3. Social control mechanisms are identical across all societies.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?"},"options":{"A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Statement 1 is correct because formal control involves written laws and institutions. Statement 2 is correct because informal control operates through customs, approval, ridicule, and shame. Statement 3 is incorrect because mechanisms of social control vary across societies and historical contexts. Therefore statements 1 and 2 are correct."},{"id":27,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Assertion (A): Conflict theorists argue that definitions of deviance are shaped by power relations.\nReason (R): Laws often reflect the interests of dominant groups in society."},"options":{"A":"Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Conflict theorists view deviance as socially constructed through unequal power relations. The reason is true because dominant groups influence lawmaking and enforcement. This directly explains the assertion. Hence option A is correct."},{"id":28,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Which of the following is the best example of secondary deviance?"},"options":{"A":"An individual commits a minor unnoticed rule violation","B":"A student accidentally breaks a classroom rule","C":"A person internalizes a deviant label and adopts a deviant identity","D":"A child imitates parental behaviour unconsciously"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Secondary deviance occurs when individuals internalize deviant labels and organize their identities around them. Options A and B are closer to primary deviance. Option D does not necessarily involve deviance. Therefore option C is correct."},{"id":29,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Consider the following statements about white-collar crime:\n1. It may produce greater economic damage than many street crimes.\n2. It is always treated with the same severity as violent crime.\n3. Corporate fraud can be classified as white-collar crime.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?"},"options":{"A":"1 and 3 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 only","D":"1, 2 and 3"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Statement 1 is correct because white-collar crimes can involve enormous financial losses. Statement 2 is incorrect because white-collar crimes are often treated differently from violent crimes. Statement 3 is correct because corporate fraud is a major form of white-collar crime. Hence statements 1 and 3 are correct."},{"id":30,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Which sociologist developed the social bond theory emphasizing attachment, commitment, involvement, and belief?"},"options":{"A":"Howard Becker","B":"Travis Hirschi","C":"Edwin Lemert","D":"Robert Merton"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Travis Hirschi developed social bond theory, arguing that strong social bonds reduce deviance. Becker is associated with labeling theory. Lemert distinguished primary and secondary deviance. Merton developed strain theory. Hence option B is correct."}]},{"section_id":"sociology_of_religion_hard","section_name":"The Sociology of Religion","section_type":"FREE_FLOAT","questions":[{"id":31,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Consider the following statements regarding Durkheim's sociology of religion:\n1. Religion distinguishes between the sacred and the profane.\n2. Totemism was studied as an elementary form of religion.\n3. Durkheim viewed religion purely as individual psychological experience.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?"},"options":{"A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 only","D":"1, 2 and 3"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Statement 1 is correct because Durkheim emphasized the distinction between sacred and profane. Statement 2 is correct because he studied totemism among Australian Aboriginal groups. Statement 3 is incorrect because Durkheim treated religion as fundamentally social rather than merely psychological. Hence statements 1 and 2 are correct."},{"id":32,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Assertion (A): Weber connected the Protestant ethic with the development of modern capitalism.\nReason (R): Weber argued that Calvinist beliefs encouraged disciplined economic activity."},"options":{"A":"Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Weber argued that Protestant values such as discipline, hard work, and asceticism contributed to capitalist development. The reason directly explains the assertion because Calvinist doctrines encouraged rational economic conduct. Therefore option A is correct."},{"id":33,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Which of the following statements about secularization is NOT correct?"},"options":{"A":"It may involve declining religious authority in public institutions","B":"It necessarily means that individuals become atheists","C":"Modernization is often associated with secularization processes","D":"Secularization can vary across societies"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Secularization does not necessarily make individuals atheists; private belief may continue even when institutional religion declines. Option A is correct because secularization affects public institutions. Option C is correct because modernization is often linked with secularization. Option D is correct because secularization differs across contexts. Therefore option B is incorrect."},{"id":34,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Match List I with List II:\nList I\n1. Civil religion\n2. Disenchantment\n3. Sacred canopy\n4. Liberation theology\n\nList II\na. Peter Berger\nb. Max Weber\nc. Robert Bellah\nd. Latin American Catholic movements"},"options":{"A":"1-c, 2-b, 3-a, 4-d","B":"1-b, 2-a, 3-d, 4-c","C":"1-d, 2-c, 3-b, 4-a","D":"1-a, 2-d, 3-c, 4-b"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Civil religion is associated with Robert Bellah. Disenchantment is a Weberian concept. Sacred canopy was developed by Peter Berger. Liberation theology emerged from Latin American Catholic activism. Hence option A is correct."},{"id":35,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Arrange the following thinkers chronologically by birth year:\n1. Karl Marx\n2. Emile Durkheim\n3. Max Weber"},"options":{"A":"2-1-3","B":"1-2-3","C":"1-3-2","D":"3-2-1"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Karl Marx was born in 1818, Emile Durkheim in 1858, and Max Weber in 1864. Therefore the correct order is 1-2-3."},{"id":36,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Consider the following statements regarding fundamentalism:\n1. It may arise as a response to perceived moral decline.\n2. Fundamentalist movements always reject modern technology.\n3. Religious fundamentalism can influence political mobilization.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?"},"options":{"A":"1 only","B":"1 and 3 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Statement 1 is correct because fundamentalist movements often respond to rapid social change and perceived moral decline. Statement 2 is incorrect because many fundamentalist groups use modern media and technology strategically. Statement 3 is correct because such movements frequently shape political action. Therefore statements 1 and 3 are correct."},{"id":37,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Assertion (A): Peter Berger initially argued that modernization would reduce the social significance of religion.\nReason (R): Berger later acknowledged that religion remained influential in many parts of the world."},"options":{"A":"Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Assertion is true because Berger initially supported secularization theory. Reason is also true because he later reconsidered the inevitability of secularization. However, the later acknowledgment does not explain the earlier argument; it revises it. Therefore option B is correct."},{"id":38,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Which concept refers to the process by which magical and religious interpretations lose influence in social life due to rationalization?"},"options":{"A":"Anomie","B":"Collective conscience","C":"Disenchantment","D":"Totemism"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Disenchantment refers to the decline of magical and mystical interpretations as rationalization expands in modern societies. Anomie concerns normlessness. Collective conscience refers to shared moral beliefs. Totemism is a religious system. Hence option C is correct."},{"id":39,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Consider the following statements about new religious movements:\n1. They often emerge during periods of social uncertainty.\n2. They are identical in structure and belief across societies.\n3. Some new religious movements emphasize charismatic leadership.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?"},"options":{"A":"1 and 3 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 only","D":"1, 2 and 3"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Statement 1 is correct because new religious movements frequently arise during social and cultural transitions. Statement 2 is incorrect because they differ significantly across societies and contexts. Statement 3 is correct because charismatic authority often plays a major role. Hence statements 1 and 3 are correct."},{"id":40,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Which sociologist developed the concept of the 'sacred canopy' to describe religion's role in providing meaning and legitimacy?"},"options":{"A":"Peter Berger","B":"Bryan Wilson","C":"Talcott Parsons","D":"Clifford Geertz"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Peter Berger used the term 'sacred canopy' to explain how religion provides a meaningful and legitimating framework for social life. Bryan Wilson focused on secularization. Parsons worked on systems theory. Geertz developed interpretive anthropology. Hence option A is correct."}]},{"section_id":"social_mobility_structural_barriers_hard","section_name":"Social Mobility and Structural Barriers","section_type":"FREE_FLOAT","questions":[{"id":41,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Consider the following statements regarding social mobility:\n1. Intergenerational mobility compares the social positions of different generations.\n2. Structural mobility may occur because of large-scale economic changes.\n3. Horizontal mobility always produces upward class movement.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?"},"options":{"A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 only","D":"1, 2 and 3"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Statement 1 is correct because intergenerational mobility compares parents and children. Statement 2 is correct because structural changes such as industrialization can alter occupational opportunities. Statement 3 is incorrect because horizontal mobility involves movement without significant status change. Therefore statements 1 and 2 are correct."},{"id":42,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Assertion (A): Bourdieu argued that schools can reproduce class inequality.\nReason (R): Educational systems often reward forms of cultural capital possessed disproportionately by privileged groups."},"options":{"A":"Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Bourdieu argued that educational institutions reproduce inequality because dominant cultural norms are treated as legitimate. The reason correctly explains how cultural capital advantages privileged groups. Therefore option A is correct."},{"id":43,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Which of the following statements about caste mobility in India is NOT correct?"},"options":{"A":"Sanskritization involves adoption of upper-caste practices","B":"Caste-based inequalities can affect educational opportunities","C":"Industrialization automatically eliminated caste distinctions everywhere","D":"Reservation policies seek to address historical disadvantages"},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Industrialization did not automatically eliminate caste distinctions. Caste continues to influence social relations and opportunities. Option A correctly defines Sanskritization. Option B is correct because caste affects access to resources and education. Option D is correct because reservation policies address historical exclusion. Therefore option C is incorrect."},{"id":44,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Match List I with List II:\nList I\n1. Social closure\n2. Cultural capital\n3. Sanskritization\n4. Habitus\n\nList II\na. Pierre Bourdieu\nb. Max Weber\nc. M. N. Srinivas\nd. Pierre Bourdieu"},"options":{"A":"1-b, 2-a, 3-c, 4-d","B":"1-a, 2-b, 3-d, 4-c","C":"1-c, 2-d, 3-b, 4-a","D":"1-d, 2-c, 3-a, 4-b"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Social closure is associated with Max Weber. Cultural capital and habitus are concepts developed by Pierre Bourdieu. Sanskritization was proposed by M. N. Srinivas. Hence option A is correct."},{"id":45,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Arrange the following concepts chronologically according to their emergence:\n1. Social closure\n2. Sanskritization\n3. Habitus"},"options":{"A":"1-2-3","B":"2-1-3","C":"1-3-2","D":"3-2-1"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Max Weber's concept of social closure emerged earliest. Sanskritization was developed by M. N. Srinivas in the mid-twentieth century. Bourdieu's concept of habitus became influential later. Therefore the correct order is 1-2-3."},{"id":46,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Consider the following statements regarding structural barriers:\n1. Gender discrimination can restrict occupational mobility.\n2. Unequal access to education may reproduce inequality.\n3. Structural barriers operate independently of economic systems.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?"},"options":{"A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Statement 1 is correct because gender discrimination can limit career advancement. Statement 2 is correct because unequal education reproduces social inequality. Statement 3 is incorrect because structural barriers are deeply connected to economic and social systems. Hence statements 1 and 2 are correct."},{"id":47,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Assertion (A): Meritocratic systems may still reproduce inequality.\nReason (R): Individuals begin competition with unequal access to resources and opportunities."},"options":{"A":"Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Critics argue that meritocracy can reproduce inequality because people do not start from equal social conditions. The reason directly explains the assertion by highlighting unequal resources and opportunities. Therefore option A is correct."},{"id":48,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Which of the following best describes social closure?"},"options":{"A":"The elimination of all class distinctions","B":"Restriction of opportunities by groups seeking to protect advantages","C":"Complete equality in access to occupations","D":"Replacement of achieved status by ascribed status"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Social closure refers to strategies used by groups to monopolize resources and opportunities by excluding others. Option A is incorrect because closure maintains inequality. Option C contradicts exclusionary practices. Option D is unrelated to Weber's concept. Hence option B is correct."},{"id":49,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Consider the following statements regarding mobility in capitalist societies:\n1. Economic restructuring can create both upward and downward mobility.\n2. Class mobility completely removes inherited inequalities.\n3. Labour market changes influence mobility patterns.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?"},"options":{"A":"1 and 3 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 only","D":"1, 2 and 3"},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Statement 1 is correct because economic change can produce both opportunities and decline. Statement 2 is incorrect because inherited inequalities often persist across generations. Statement 3 is correct because labour markets shape occupational mobility. Therefore statements 1 and 3 are correct."},{"id":50,"type":"MCQ","language":{"en":"Which sociologist is most closely associated with the concepts of habitus, field, and symbolic capital?"},"options":{"A":"Talcott Parsons","B":"Pierre Bourdieu","C":"Kingsley Davis","D":"Ralf Dahrendorf"},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Pierre Bourdieu developed the concepts of habitus, field, cultural capital, and symbolic capital to explain the reproduction of inequality. Parsons focused on structural functionalism. Davis worked on stratification theory. Dahrendorf emphasized authority and conflict. Hence option B is correct."}]}]}
